1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,500
Look closely at this California spiny lobster.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:11,000
Those long antennae and the spiny shell are clues: unlike the familiar clawed lobster, this species has no big front pincers.

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:16,000
NOAA filmed these animals among the rocky reefs of California’s Channel Islands.

4
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
By day, California spiny lobsters often shelter in reef crevices.

5
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
After dark, they emerge to search for food.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
They are not just scavengers: their diet can include sea urchins, small clams, mussels and worms.

7
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
And when danger comes close, a powerful flick of the tail can send a lobster swimming backwards.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:55,000
A hidden reef resident by day. A night-time forager after dark. That is the California spiny lobster.
